6. Product Experience

6.1 Shared Entry Journey

All clients begin with the same two steps:

Select Project Sector
→ Select or Explore a Relevant Service

Each service is presented through real visual examples so the client can understand its possibilities without needing specialist terminology.

After selecting a service, TENTORA presents two clear actions:

Client Intent

Product Path

“I need something now and want to customize it myself.”

Design Now

“I want a creative to design it for me.”

Work With a Creative

The paths remain connected. A client may begin with a ready design and later transfer the saved work, content, and preferences to a creative for professional development.

6.2 Experience Principle

TENTORA should minimize required input at the beginning. It should not force users to complete a long project questionnaire before they can understand the available services or see creative work.

Project details such as budget, deadline, deliverables, and technical requirements should be requested only when they become necessary for purchasing, matching, or execution.

7. Path A: Design Now

7.1 Purpose

Design Now is the fast self-service path for clients who want an immediate result without beginning a full creative project.

The user does not begin with a blank canvas, complete a full taste test, or wait for creative recommendations. Instead, the user selects a ready interactive design related to the chosen service and customizes it directly.

7.2 Journey

Select Sector
→ Select Service
→ Browse Ready Interactive Designs
→ Select a Design
→ Customize Content and Visual Properties
→ Preview
→ Export or Purchase

Example:

Food and Beverage
→ Menu Design
→ Select a Ready Menu
→ Add Items and Prices
→ Adjust Type, Colours, Images, and Layout
→ Preview
→ Export

7.3 Ready Interactive Designs

Ready designs are structured creative systems rather than flat images. Depending on the service, editable properties may include:

Text and content.

Typefaces and type scale.

Colours and brand tokens.

Images and illustrations.

Layout options.

Motion speed and behaviour.

Format, dimensions, and output channel.

Logo and brand-asset placement.

Ready designs may be created by:

Curated TENTORA creatives.

Selected creative studios.

The internal TENTORA creative team.

Collaborations between TENTORA and independent creatives.

The original creative remains identified and compensated according to the design's commercial model.

7.4 AI Within Design Now

AI accelerates customization inside an existing design system. It does not have to generate the complete design from an empty canvas.

Depending on the service, AI may:

Reflow a layout according to content length.

Suggest or apply colours compatible with an uploaded logo.

Remove image backgrounds.

Improve product images.

Generate a missing visual element when permitted.

Resize and adapt a design for multiple channels.

Translate or rewrite content.

Create consistent variations from the selected design.

Check readability, spacing, hierarchy, and visual balance.

7.5 Moving From Self-Service to a Creative

If the client needs deeper customization or professional development, the current work can be transferred to a creative without restarting the journey.

The transfer may include:

The selected ready design.

Client content and uploaded assets.

Saved customizations.

Preferred colours, type, and visual properties.

Exported previews.

A structured summary generated from the editing activity.

This creates a connected journey:

Customize → Save → Continue With a Creative

8. Path B: Work With a Creative

8.1 Purpose

Work With a Creative is the path for clients who want an original, professionally developed, or multi-service creative solution.

8.2 Journey

Select Sector
→ Explore Services and Creative Work
→ Select One or Multiple Services
→ Complete One Visual Taste Test
→ Apply One Main Taste Profile Across the Project
→ Optionally Refine the Direction of a Specific Service
→ Receive Up to Six Creative or Team Recommendations
→ Explore Portfolios and Select the Best Match
→ Manage the Project
→ Final Delivery

8.3 Multi-Service Project Needs

The client can add one or multiple services to My Project Needs. All selected services remain inside one project context.

This prevents clients from starting a separate discovery and matching journey for every service.

8.4 One Taste Test Per Project

The client completes one image-based visual taste test. TENTORA converts the selections into a structured Main Taste Profile and applies it to all selected services by default.

The Main Taste Profile may include:

Visual style.

Colour preferences.

Mood and atmosphere.

Materials and textures.

Shape language.

Level of simplicity or boldness.

Artistic direction.

If the client wants a different direction for one service, the client may select Customize This Service Direction. TENTORA retains the main taste as a starting point and presents a shorter, service-specific visual refinement instead of repeating the full test.

The taste model supports:

Main Taste Profile: the default direction for the project.

Service Taste Direction: an optional variation for one service.

Additional Taste Direction: an optional alternative when comparing more than one style for the same service.

8.5 Creative and Team Recommendations

TENTORA returns a maximum of six recommendation options to avoid recreating endless profile browsing.

A recommendation may represent:

One creative for one service.

One multidisciplinary creative or studio for several services.

One compatible creative team when several specialists are required.

For multi-service projects, TENTORA prioritizes options that preserve visual consistency. If one creative cannot cover all selected services, the platform may recommend a coordinated team whose Creative DNA profiles align with the project and with one another.

8.6 Selection and Project Workspace

The client explores the recommended portfolios and selects the preferred creative or team. Each recommendation should explain:

Why the option was selected.

Taste compatibility.

Relevant services.

Sector experience.

Similar portfolio work.

Services covered by the creative or team.

After selection, TENTORA provides a project workspace that may include:

Project stages and timeline.

Messages.

References and files.

Feedback and revision cycles.

Approvals.

Team responsibilities.

Deliverables.

Delivery tracking.

9. Core Product Components

9.1 Sector-Based Service Discovery

The sector structures the available services and prevents irrelevant content from appearing. Each service includes curated visual examples and clear access to both product paths.

9.2 Ready Design System

Each ready design requires structured editable properties, preview states, output formats, authorship, pricing, licensing, and customization limits.

The system should distinguish between:

Properties editable directly by the client.

Properties adjustable with AI assistance.

Changes that require the original creative.

Changes that are not permitted under the design licence.

9.3 Taste Engine

The Taste Engine converts visual choices into a structured Taste Profile. The MVP may begin with weighted tags, while later versions can introduce image embeddings and learned preference models.

9.4 Creative DNA

Each creative has a structured Creative DNA derived from portfolio work and professional information. It may include:

Creative specialty and services.

Visual styles and artistic characteristics.

Relevant sectors and project experience.

Portfolio taste attributes.

Single-service and multi-service capabilities.

Availability and preferred project scale.

Location when relevant.

Pricing level.

Ready designs offered by the creative.

Creative DNA allows TENTORA to evaluate real visual identity and capability rather than relying only on a job title or keyword.

9.5 Matching Engine

The matching engine compares:

Project sector.

Selected services.

Main Taste Profile.

Optional service-specific directions.

Creative DNA.

Multi-service coverage.

Team compatibility when required.

The MVP may use weighted tag overlap and cosine similarity. Later versions can incorporate image embeddings, behavioural signals, completed-project outcomes, and machine-learning models.

9.6 Project Workspace

The workspace connects discovery and delivery. It stores the selected services, taste direction, recommended or selected creatives, references, files, communication, feedback, approvals, milestones, and final deliverables.

9.7 Relational Data Model

The platform must support relationships between:

Sectors and services.

Services and ready designs.

Ready designs and editable properties.

Ready designs and their original creatives.

Clients and projects.

Projects and selected services.

Projects and Taste Profiles.

Services and optional taste directions.

Creatives, portfolios, and Creative DNA.

Recommendations, teams, and team members.

Project workspace activity and deliverables.

10. Role of Artificial Intelligence

AI operates as a core intelligence and customization layer within TENTORA. It is used where it reduces time, improves relevance, simplifies visual decisions, or connects self-service activity with professional execution.

Its responsibilities include:

Interactive Design Customization: adapting ready designs to client content, assets, formats, and brand properties.

Visual Taste Extraction: interpreting selected images and, in later phases, uploaded references.

Taste Structuring: converting visual selections into reusable project and service taste directions.

Creative Matching: comparing project needs and taste data with Creative DNA.

Multi-Service Consistency: identifying creatives or team combinations capable of maintaining a coherent direction.

Creative Handoff: converting self-service editing activity into a structured summary for a professional creative.

Project Assistance: organizing briefs, feedback, revisions, files, and next actions.

Continuous Improvement: learning from saved designs, edits, rejected options, selected creatives, feedback, and completed projects.

AI is a major part of the product experience, but the final artistic judgment, original authorship, professional craft, and custom execution remain human where a creative is involved.

11. Human Creativity and Creative Ownership

TENTORA should create value for creatives rather than use their work as unprotected input.

For every ready design, the platform should define:

Original creator attribution.

Ownership of the source design.

Permitted customization.

AI-assisted changes that are allowed.

Usage licence.

Whether the design is sold once or licensed repeatedly.

Revenue share or compensation.

Whether further changes require the original creative.

The platform should not use a creative's portfolio or ready designs for model training without clear permission and appropriate terms.

Creatives may generate revenue through:

Ready-design purchases or licences.

Paid customization requests.

Original commissioned projects.

Multi-service or team projects.

12. Unique Value Proposition

TENTORA transforms fragmented creative discovery into two connected outcomes: an immediate customizable result or an original professionally executed solution.

Its value is built on six principles:

Begin with the project sector and service, not an open profile directory.

Show services through real creative work.

Give urgent clients a fast ready-design path.

Use one Taste Profile across one or multiple custom services.

Limit matching results to a small and relevant set of creatives or teams.

Allow clients to move from self-service design to professional execution without restarting.

TENTORA is not a conventional freelance marketplace, a generic template library, or an AI image generator. It is an AI-powered creative-solutions platform connecting ready interactive design, visual taste, human creative expertise, and project delivery.

13. Potential Impact

TENTORA can reduce the time, uncertainty, and visual inconsistency involved in obtaining creative work.

For clients, it can:

Provide immediate usable results for simple or urgent needs.

Make creative services easier to understand visually.

Reduce unnecessary profile browsing.

Improve the relevance of creative recommendations.

Preserve one visual direction across multiple services.

Keep custom projects organized through delivery.

For creatives, it can:

Create recurring revenue from ready designs.

Generate better-fit custom opportunities.

Preserve attribution and ownership.

Convert self-service users into professional customization clients.

Present portfolio work within relevant sectors and services.

For the platform, each interaction can create structured signals about selected services, design choices, edits, saved results, taste preferences, rejected recommendations, and successful matches. With appropriate consent and governance, these signals can improve personalization and matching over time.

